      "fullText": "Prison advocacy in the twenty-first century represents a paradigmatic shift from punitive incarceration to constitutional correction, human dignity, and social reintegration. In India, the prison system remains overburdened with an occupancy rate of  131.4% and under trial prisoners constituting 75.8% of  total inmates (National Crime Records Bureau [NCRB], 2022). This structural crisis exposes deep fissures between constitutional guarantees under Articles 14, 19, 21 and 39A and ground realities of  access to Crime, Justice & Society, critically examines the historical evolution of  prison reforms, contemporary challenges of  overcrowding, legal aid deficits, and socio-legal exclusion of  vulnerable groups including women, children, persons with mental illness, and Scheduled Castes and Tribes. Drawing upon doctrinal and empirical analysis, the paper conceptualizes prison advocacy as a rights-based, multi- stakeholder intervention encompassing free legal aid, timely bail facilitation under Section 479 of Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS) 2023, legal literacy, psychosocial support including stress and anger management, and rehabilitation planning. The article presents a detailed case study of  the Prison Advocacy Project at National Law University Odisha (NLUO), Cuttack, launched in 2024 in collaboration with Azim Premji Foundation, which has within one year interacted with 881 under trial prisoners, secured bail for 90 prisoners (60 free of  cost), provided legal assistance in 28 cases, and conducted 52 legal awareness sessions covering 13 topics benefiting over 2000 inmates across Choudwar Circle Jail, Banki Sub-Jail, Bhubaneswar Special Jail and Khordha District Jail. The Banki Sub-Jail witnessed significant reduction in population, demonstrating efficacy of  timely legal intervention. The paper argues that such academic-civil society-state partnerships must be institutionalized nationally as part of  Sustainable Development Goal 16 and Digital Social Citizenship framework. Keywords: Prison Advocacy, Under trial Prisoners, Legal Aid, Criminal Justice Reform, Rehabilitation, NLUO, Article 21, BNSS, SDG 16, Digital Social Citizenship"
