          "Overall, the study had some limitations. This was largely due to the use of secondary research and data analysis. By only using pre-existing data, gaps in the research emerged, which made it difficult to thoroughly apply the data to the research question. Since the research question was framed within the context of white women and Indigenous women, and the pre-existing data did not break down the quantities of missing women based on race, it was difficult to comprehensively analyze the data. This means that some limitations arose when trying to directly compare the number of news stories with the number of reported missing women based on race. There was data on the number of missing people in Canada in 2024 who were Indigenous. As well, it was indicated what percentage of these missing Indigenous people were women. So, while they did not supply specific datasets on what percentage of the total amount of missing people were represented by Indigenous women, this was roughly calculable by combining the percentages provided. Thus, while the use of secondary data is helpful in getting a general idea of the statistics on missing people in Canada in 2024, the lack of specificity about how many missing women were Indigenous, white, or other races created limitations for the research. It was not feasible to assume that the number of missing women subtracted by the number of missing Indigenous women equalled the number of missing white women because this would not account for the possibility of missing women in Canada who were neither white nor Indigenous. Further, the data supplied by Statistics Canada is not comprehensive, as a vast majority of missing persons cases in Canada go unreported both to the police and by the police to government information collection agencies. In fact, according to the National Centre for Missing Persons and Unidentified Remains, anywhere from \u201c25%-75% of missing persons reports made to police are not entered into the Canadian Police Information System\u201d (Canada\u2019s Missing 2024, para.12), resulting in discrepancies between the number of missing person cases opened by police and the number reported to the government. Qualitatively, there are some limitations with the findings due to the number of articles used. It was not possible within the confines of the project to thoroughly investigate every article written about missing women in 2024 and 2025. Furthermore, barriers to finding articles arose because not every article was retrieved by the algorithm. This was difficult to get around. Thus, the qualitative findings are not comprehensive of all the articles written across 2024 and 2025 on the subject."
