      "background": "The prevention of terrorism and cyberterrorism increasingly depends on the coordination of security institutions, intelligence capabilities, and digital resilience in an interconnected global threat matrix.",
      "purpose": "This article examines how a National Security Strategy can effectively integrate the prevention of physical and digital terrorism while vigorously preserving the rule of law and constitutional safeguards.",
      "methods": "Adopting an interdisciplinary legal-dogmatic approach, the paper analyzes European and Portuguese constitutional frameworks, international defense doctrines, and intelligence-led crime prevention mechanisms.",
      "results": "Reveals that blurred distinctions between intelligence surveillance and criminal prosecution risk fundamental right violations unless strict jurisdictional boundaries and judicial oversight are preserved.",
      "conclusion": "Recommends an integrated national security doctrine establishing clear statutory firewalls between intelligence gathering and evidential standards alongside proactive critical infrastructure cyber resilience.",
      "fullText": "Background: The prevention of  terrorism and cyberterrorism increasingly depends on the coordination of  security institutions, intelligence capabilities and digital resilience. Global interdependence and the use of  cyberspace complicate the distinction between internal and external threats, while predictive technologies raise questions about legality, evidence and accountability. Purpose: This article examines how a National Security Strategy can integrate the prevention of  terrorism and cyberterrorism while preserving the rule of  law and fundamental rights. It argues that effective prevention requires institutional coordination without collapsing the distinct functions of  intelligence, cybersecurity, national defence, criminal investigation and judicial review. Methodology: The article adopts a legal-dogmatic and interdisciplinary approach. It analyses the Portuguese constitutional and statutory framework, relevant national and European strategic instruments, and selected scholarship in security studies, intelligence studies and crime prevention. The analysis distinguishes threat, risk, intelligence and evidence and assesses preventive measures against legal basis, institutional competence, evidential status, necessity, proportionality and accountability. Findings and implications: Intelligence-led policing, predictive analytics and artificial intelligence can improve early warning and prioritisation, but their outputs remain probabilistic and cannot independently establish criminal responsibility or justify coercive measures. The article proposes a boundary-oriented model in which information-sharing and operational cooperation are paired with clear mandates, meaningful human oversight, data-protection safeguards and reviewable decisions. It concludes that a coherent national- security strategy should connect internal security, defence, intelligence and cybersecurity while preserving their legal and institutional distinctions. This approach can strengthen preparedness in strategic planning and operational practice without turning preventive security into indiscriminate surveillance or treating algorithmic assessments as evidence. Keywords: Terrorism, Cyberterrorism, National Security, Prevention; Intelligence, Cybersecurity, Fundamental Rights, Artificial Intelligence"
