          "The Portuguese Constitution (CRP) defines, among the State\u2019s fundamental tasks, the safeguarding of national independence, the protection of fundamental rights and guarantees, and respect for the principles of the democratic rule of law. At the same time, 34 it should be recalled that, following Max Weber\u2019s sociological definition, the State is a CRP , art.9.34",
          "human community that successfully claims, within a given territory, the monopoly of the legitimate use of physical force. 35 Likewise, the State is responsible for guaranteeing the rights to liberty and security, which are, broadly speaking, safeguarded by the police pursuant to Article 272 of the CRP , particularly with regard to upholding democratic legality and guaranteeing internal security and citizens\u2019 rights. The police function is also expressed through police measures and 36 crime prevention, both of which are grounded in the principles of legality and proportionality. 37 The Constitution does not itself define the structure of the police in the broad sense; this structure is established by legislation governing the organisation of the Security Forces (SF), which falls within the exclusive legislative competence of the Assembly of the Republic. 38 The Internal Security Law (ISL), approved by Law No. 53/2008 of 29 August, establishes the structure and functions of Portugal\u2019s Internal Security System (ISS). It defines internal security as State activity to guarantee public order, security and tranquility; protect persons and property; prevent and suppress crime; and support democratic institutions, fundamental rights and democratic legality. The law also makes clear that internal-security activity must comply with the Constitution and legislation and that police measures must be necessary, adequate and proportionate. Against this legal background, it is important to recall that, over the last two decades, and particularly since the end of the Cold War, security studies have been enriched by research into the social processes contributing to the construction of new security objects, including the nature of threats, the social construction of the idea of the enemy, feelings of insecurity, and the perception of risk and vulnerability. Likewise, the contemporary globalised world presents security threats 39 characterised by their transnational nature, reflecting the fact that no State can address them alone and that no State is immune to the risks to which they relate, together with an increasing reliance on international agencies and bilateral mechanisms and potentially greater coordination capacity. 40 Louren\u00e7o & Costa (2018, p. 35).35 CRP , art. 27.36 CRP , art. 272.37 CRP , art. 272.38 Law No. 53/2008, of August 29 (Internal Security Law), in its current wording.39 No. 1 and 3 of article 1 of the LSI.40",
          "Recent geopolitical tensions, including Russia-NATO confrontation and renewed strategic competition, contribute to an uncertain security environment. Such developments matter to national planning, but should be assessed alongside transnational terrorism, cyber threats and domestic vulnerabilities rather than treated as a substitute for risk analysis. 41 The foregoing calls for a broader theorization of national security. Realist approaches traditionally define national security through territorial integrity, sovereignty and protection against external threats, while assigning internal security a role in maintaining public order and protecting institutions. Contemporary interdependence makes this division incomplete: cyber operations, transnational networks and critical- infrastructure dependencies can produce effects across both domains. A national-security strategy should therefore integrate these dimensions without erasing their distinct legal mandates. National security can be analysed through distinct but connected functions: national defence and military security; intelligence concerning threats to State interests; internal security and law enforcement; and human security, including civil protection and the protection of people. These functions overlap in practice but remain subject to different legal mandates and accountability mechanisms. An integrated strategy should coordinate them without merging their competences. Beck\u2019s account of the \u201crisk society\u201d helps explain how technological, environmental and transnational risks cross borders and expose local vulnerabilities. This perspective supports a multidimensional approach to national security rather than a narrow focus on military threats. 42 In 2013, the Strategic Concept of National Defence (SCND) was approved. It defined \u201cthe fundamental aspects of the overall strategy to be adopted by the State for the achievement of the objectives of national security and defence policy\u201d. The SCND refers expressly to 43 the concept of national security, although the previous 2003 SCND had already addressed it, albeit less explicitly. The current SCND approaches the ISL as an integral part of a broader and more comprehensive National Security system, advocating cooperation between the Armed Forces (AF) and the Security Forces and Services (SFS) in combating transnational threats. In this respect, it is important to emphasise that the SCND is provided for in Article 7 of Law No. 31-A/2009 of 7 July and that, under paragraph 1 of that provision, it defines \u201cthe State\u2019s priorities in the field of defence, according to the national interest\u201d and forms an integral part of national defence policy. Accordingly, it concerns State priorities in the field of defence rather than the domain of internal security. Louren\u00e7o & Costa (2018, p. 80).41 Louren\u00e7o & Costa (2018, p. 80).42 Louren\u00e7o & Costa (2018, p. 28).43",
          "Several European states, including Spain, France and the United Kingdom, have adopted national-security strategies that coordinate public institutions and, where relevant, private actors. These examples provide comparative context for considering an integrated Portuguese framework. There is therefore a case for developing a National Security Strategy that encompasses internal security and national defence, while remaining aligned with the applicable European security strategy. Moreover, any such strategy must respond to contemporary challenges, which are constantly changing. For the purposes of this article, the expression \u201cNational Security Strategy\u201d is used as a proposed integrating strategic framework rather than as a claim that Portugal currently possesses a single comprehensive statutory strategy bearing that exact institutional status. The argument is therefore normative and architectural: it concerns how the relevant security, defence, intelligence and cybersecurity instruments could be coordinated within a coherent strategic framework, while remaining subject to the competences and legal limits established by the Constitution and ordinary legislation. Automated analysis of biographical, image, voice and other identifying data can assist counter-terrorism work where information volumes exceed manual capacity. Its use nevertheless requires a lawful purpose, reliable provenance, access controls and safeguards against unjustified interference with rights. Automation may improve the processing of large datasets, but it does not remove the need for human assessment, necessity and proportionality. Analytical outputs should inform, not independently determine, preventive or coercive decisions. Any national strategy concerning internal security should therefore incorporate and remain coherent with relevant European and national strategies. In particular, it should be consistent with the European Union Internal Security Strategy, the EU strategy for the eradication of trafficking in human beings, the EU drugs strategy, the EU cybersecurity strategy, and the EU strategy for combating terrorist radicalization and recruitment. 44"
