          "Within the Portuguese Internal Security System (ISS), internal security encompasses \u201cthree guarantees \u2013 public order, security and tranquility \u2013, two protections \u2013 persons and property \u2013, one preventive function \u2013 crime prevention \u2013 and three contributions \u2013 the normal functioning of democratic institutions, the regular exercise of citizens fundamental rights, freedoms and guarantees, and respect for democratic legality\u201d. 45 Accordingly, security-policy planning pursues three fundamental objectives: \u201c(1) first, preventing symmetrical and asymmetrical risks from materializing; then, (2) protecting people, democratic institutions, critical infrastructures and security services and forces; and, finally, (3) containing the impacts and/or effects of a catastrophic event, managing its Council of Ministers Resolution No. 19/2013.44 Gra\u00e7a (2017, p. 112); Carvalho (2015, p. 112).45",
          "consequences, restoring order and law, and facilitating the return to the conditions of normality that existed before the crisis\u201d. 46 Security is a broad public function whose boundaries with defence and justice must be clearly defined. Coordination is necessary, but institutional roles and legal powers must remain distinct. During a lawfully declared state of emergency, the Armed Forces may 47 support civilian authorities within the constitutional and statutory framework. Such involvement should remain limited to the authorized purpose and duration of the emergency. The traditional Westphalian distinction treated borders as separating internal 48 from external security. Global interdependence has weakened the explanatory value of geography alone, as threats and their effects increasingly cross-national boundaries. 49 Internal and external security are now mutually dependent: globalization externalizes domestic problems and internalizes external ones. Security analysis must therefore consider cross-border causes, actors and consequences. Brand\u00e3o identifies three dynamics linking 50 the two dimensions: the internalization of externally generated phenomena, the externalization of domestic phenomena, and the growth of transboundary threats. 51 Internal security can no longer be understood simply as a domestic version of external security. Their relationship is more complex and reflects interdependence across national and international settings. Under the CRP , a distinction exists between National Defence 52 (ND) and Internal Security (IS), corresponding to the classical distinction of modern States \u201cwhich, since the fragmentation of the empires of Central Europe (\u2026) continues to inform even the most scholastic conception of security strategies: in the face of external threats, taking territorial borders as the term or limit, ND falls essentially to the Armed Forces; in relation to threats within those borders, maintaining IS falls to the police forces\u201d. The Armed Forces\u2019 primary mission remains national defence, but transnational 53 and hybrid threats can require structured cooperation with civilian security institutions. Such cooperation must rest on constitutional and statutory authority, defined command arrangements and clear limits on the functions performed by each body. Integration should Almeida (2013, p. 7). Nogueira (2012) 46 Almeida (2013, p. 8).47 Almeida (2013, p. 8).48 Guedes (2015a).49 Gra\u00e7a (2017, p. 19); Collins (2016, p. 2); Collins (2007, p. 2)50 Brand\u00e3o (2015, p. 5-6); Brand\u00e3o (2014, p. 5-6).51 Guedes (2015b).52 Servi\u00e7o de Informa\u00e7\u00f5es de Seguran\u00e7a (2019, p. 4-5).53",
          "improve preparedness and exchange information, not blur the distinction between military defence, police powers and intelligence activity. In this context, particular importance should be attached to the protocol signed on 28 February 2020 containing the Guidelines for Operational Coordination between the Armed Forces and the Security Forces and Services. The protocol seeks to establish, at 54 operational level, how cooperation between these State entities is to be implemented in matters of internal security. Its adoption reflects the necessary adaptation of these institutions to new threats, including terrorism, organized crime, cyber threats, cyberterrorism and protection-and-rescue operations. The historical distinction between internal and external security has become less clear because threats, institutions and non-state actors operate across both domains. This development supports an integrated national-security perspective while preserving legal distinctions between institutional mandates. Finally, attention should be drawn to the 55 need for and relevance of creating a Strategic Concept of Internal Security (SCIS), which should strategically develop \u201cside by side\u201d with the SCND. Several proposals have already been formulated. The SCIS has been presented as an \u201cessential component in defining the architecture of a National Security System that integrates, through a logic of complementarity, the systems of military security, internal security, intelligence and civil protection, without neglecting other important political, economic and social factors that contribute to achieving national-security strategic objectives\u201d. The proposed security 56 architecture is based on a coherent and coordinated Internal Security System, with complementary roles for public institutions and relevant private actors. Coordination should support shared situational awareness and resource prioritisation while preserving the accountability and competence of each participating institution. Proposals for reform have included stronger political coordination, clearer operational command arrangements and a coordination centre to reduce friction between actors, standardize procedures and support joint operations. Any such model must remain consistent with statutory competences and accountability. 57 Sistema de Seguran\u00e7a Interna (2020a). Orienta\u00e7\u00f5es para a articula\u00e7\u00e3o operacional entre as For\u00e7as Armadas e as For\u00e7as e 54 Servi\u00e7os de Seguran\u00e7a. Collins (2016, p. 3); Collins (2007, p. 3)55 Moleirinho (2009, p. 280).56 Louren\u00e7o et al. (2015, p. 83-84).57",
