          "The contemporary national-security paradigm combines policy coordination, legal measures and international cooperation. It reflects the fact that domestic security decisions may affect regional and global peace and security. Its development may therefore be structured around four pillars that reinforce the notion of National Security: \u201cNational Defence and the Armed Forces; Internal Security and the Police Forces; State Security and the Intelligence Services; Community Security and Civil Protection\u201d. Beck\u2019s \u201crisk society\u201d highlights how technological and urban development 59 coexist with complex risks that cross political, economic and social boundaries. Security analysis must therefore account for multidimensional vulnerabilities rather than military threats alone. Contemporary risks are networked, adaptable and capable of affecting 60 individual well-being and security at local, national and international levels. 61 States consequently need responses that are dynamic, coordinated, integrated and multidisciplinary. 62 Security policy now extends beyond military threats to include crises such as natural disasters, pandemics and food insecurity, whose effects may undermine social resilience. 63 Portugal\u2019s position at the European Union\u2019s external border gives internal-security policy a clear European dimension. National measures must therefore align with EU cooperation in the Area of Freedom, Security and Justice and with relevant common strategies, while reflecting Portugal\u2019s own constitutional allocation of responsibilities. This reinforces the case for a national-security framework that connects internal security, defence, intelligence and cybersecurity, rather than treating them as isolated policy fields. Portugal\u2019s EU membership means that national security choices also affect European cooperation and shared security objectives. Louren\u00e7o et al. (2015, p. 86).58 Gouveia (2018, p. 367).59 Beck (2009, p. 19).60 Elias (2011, p. 147).61 Sousa (2008).62 Buzan (2007, p. 24)63",
          "Several states have adopted cross-government national security strategies that coordinate public institutions and, where appropriate, private actors. Such examples offer comparative context, but the Portuguese model must be designed around its own constitutional allocation of powers and statutory institutions. Conceptual confusion between security and defence can obstruct cooperation and encourage institutional competition, weakening responses to transnational threats. 64 National security is best understood as an integrated framework connecting the institutions, powers and instruments that protect the State and society. A \u201csystem of systems\u201d 65 approach coordinates different instruments of State power and analyses the causes, purposes and dynamics of threats before selecting responses. Such a strategy should be 66 structural in nature and make the Portuguese ISS better able to address present and future challenges. Its principal lines of action should include the following. The proposed framework would connect four principal pillars: State security and intelligence; civil protection and human security; internal security and criminal investigation in cooperation with judicial authorities; and national defence, including legally authorized subsidiary support. The pillars should have distinct functions: defence addresses military security, 67 intelligence supports State security, civil protection covers emergencies and human security, and internal security is primarily delivered by the competent security forces and services. Coordination should occur at governmental level without erasing these distinctions. 68 A National Security Strategy should guide policies across diplomatic, intelligence, military, internal-security, economic and cultural domains, including the protection of national cohesion and identity. Alongside defence, internal security, foreign policy and intelligence, 69 relevant areas include civil protection, food and maritime security, cybersecurity, public health and cooperation with private actors and civil society. The Strategic Reflection 70 Group on Security proposed a National Security Law and a Superior Council for National Security to coordinate implementation, crisis monitoring and strategic planning. 71 Silva & Ribeiro (2018, p. 149).64 Gouveia (2018, p. 365).65 Louren\u00e7o et al. (2015, p. 60-61).66 Louren\u00e7o et al. (2015, p. 60-61).67 Moleirinho (2009, p. 279)68 Louren\u00e7o et al. (2015, p. 64).69 Louren\u00e7o et al. (2015, p. 66).70 Louren\u00e7o & Costa (2018); Louren\u00e7o et al. (2015).71",
          "The proposal combines a National Security Strategy, a National Security Law, a Superior Council and a consultative council. The proposal responds to the multidimensional nature 72 of contemporary societal change and security risks. Current threats involve diverse state and non-state actors, asymmetric methods and new technological capabilities, especially in cyberspace. Hybrid campaigns may combine informational, economic, energy, technological and political instruments to exploit vulnerabilities and weaken institutions. Although conventional military action remains relevant, security environments increasingly include drones, cyberattacks, special forces and remote capabilities. Non-state actors may also use asymmetric violence that disregards humanitarian norms. At domestic level, relevant concerns include jihadist terrorism, transnational organized crime, violence and potential social instability, particularly in metropolitan areas. The broadened concept of security places individuals and societal, political and economic conditions alongside the State, while recognising that many contemporary threats originate abroad without amounting to armed conflict. The GRES study favors a coordinated \u201csystem of systems\u201d, with autonomous but interdependent strategic concepts guided by complementarity and subsidiarity, and prevention policies that address both insecurity and its public perception. 73"
