          "Preventing terrorism and cyberterrorism within a National Security Strategy requires an integrated approach capable of articulating intelligence production, early warning, cooperation among security forces and services, criminal police bodies, the Public Prosecution Service, cybersecurity and cyber-defence structures, and other relevant public and private actors, always within their respective legal competences. Law No. 59/2019, of August 8, which transposes Directive (EU) 2016/680 into the Portuguese legal system; Article 2(3) 118 excludes the processing of personal data related to national security. Law No. 33/2026, of July 21st, which amends Law No. 52/2003, of August 22nd (Combating Terrorism Law); Decree-119 Law No. 125/2025, of December 4, which approves the legal regime for cybersecurity and transposes Directive (EU) 2022/2555.",
          "Such integration does not, however, eliminate the functional boundaries between the different security systems. Intelligence has an essentially prospective and anticipatory function; criminal investigation seeks to establish facts, identify offenders and obtain evidence; cybersecurity and cyber-defence, according to their respective legal and institutional frameworks, protect and enhance the resilience of systems and infrastructures; and judicial decision-making retains its constitutional autonomy. Cooperation must therefore coexist with a clear differentiation of competences. Accordingly, the preventive use of analytical technologies should be assessed through a cumulative sequence: whether the capability is technically and operationally relevant; whether there is a sufficient legal basis for its use; which institution is competent to act; what evidential status the resulting information has; whether the contemplated measure is necessary, suitable and proportionate; and whether the decision remains subject to meaningful institutional and legal accountability. In the field of terrorism and cyberterrorism, this distinction is particularly relevant. Not every cyberattack constitutes cyberterrorism, just as the use of digital means by a terrorist organisation does not automatically transform every digital activity into an act of cyberterrorism. Legal classification depends on the material and subjective elements required by the applicable legal regime. Prevention must also distinguish threat, risk, intent, preparation and evidence, avoiding the conversion of prospective assessments into individual imputations without additional grounds. The use of predictive policing, intelligence-led policing and artificial intelligence may strengthen analytical and anticipatory capacity, but it does not eliminate uncertainty or replace legally responsible human decision-making. Probabilistic risk estimates do not amount to criminal evidence, and statistical correlations do not, in themselves, establish individual authorship. The use of these tools must comply with legality, necessity, suitability and proportionality, as well as applicable requirements concerning data protection, human oversight, transparency and accountability. At institutional level, effective prevention depends on coherence among national instruments relating to internal security, national defence, intelligence, cybersecurity and cyber-defence. The current legal framework reinforces the need for coordination and resilience without eliminating the functional autonomy of the entities involved. The objective should not be to concentrate all preventive capabilities in a single entity, but to build cooperation mechanisms capable of sharing relevant information, reducing vulnerabilities and ensuring proportionate responses to different categories of threat. Effective prevention integrates information and action while preserving legality, proportionality, evidence and accountability. A strategy should improve cooperation without treating intelligence assessments as proof or allowing technology to determine coercive decisions."
