          "global species to extinction, and predicted the Earth's temperature to warm to 1.5\u00b0C by 2 2030 to 2052. Indonesia is a mega-biodiverse country that has a strategic role at the global 3 level due to its vast tropical forests. Indonesia has 120,4 million hectares of forest area and 5,3 million hectares of marine protected areas. Indonesia's tropical forests are a global 4 climate regulator and have high biodiversity. According to the Indonesian Biodiversity Strategy and Action Plan/IBSAP 2025-2045, Indonesia is home to 31.902 flora species, 81.260 terrestrial fauna species, and 7.841 marine fauna species. Biodiversity has miscellaneous ecosystem functions, including carbon sequestration, water storage and purification, decomposition, storage and recycling of nutrients, and the provision of oxygen, clean water, fertile soil, food, protection from disasters such as floods and fires, climate regulation, and medicines. In fact, the rate of biodiversity loss in 5 Indonesia is alarming. Indonesia has 583 endangered species, including 191 species of mammals and 160 species of birds. Research also reported that 490 Indonesian plant 6 species are threatened. 7 The sustainability of ecosystems is threatened by wildlife crime. These crimes include directly harming or killing wildlife, taking wildlife from the wild, possessing, selling, or exploiting wildlife. Wildlife crime is one of the main factors causing wildlife extinction in 8 the last two decades. This crime leads to disruption of food chains, food webs, pollination, 9 seed dispersal, pest populations, widespread environmental changes, and human health impacts due to zoonoses. Globally, the value of money turnover of wildlife crime reaches E. Turnhout and A. Purvis, \u201cBiodiversity and species extinction: categorisation, calculation, and communication\u201d, 2 Griffith Law Review 2020 29(4): 669\u2013685. https://doi.org/10.1080/10383441.2020.1925204. Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), \u201cSummary for Policymakers\u201d, In Climate Change 2021 \u2013 The 3 Physical Science Basis: Working Group I Contribution to the Sixth Assessment Report of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2023), 15. Ministry of Environment and Forestry, The State of Indonesia\u2019s Forests 2024 - Towards Sustainability of Forest Ecosystems 4 in Indonesia (Jakarta: Ministry of Environment and Forestry, 2024), 14. E. S. Nelson and D. R. Reddy, Green Aviation - Reduction of Environmental Impact Through Aircraft Technology and 5 Alternative Fuels (London: Taylor & Francis Group, 2017). 327. Agus Setiawan, \u201cIndonesia's Biodiversity: Problems and Conservation Efforts\u201d, Indonesian Journal of Conservation 6 11(1) (2022): 13-21. DOI 10.15294/ijc.v11i1.34532. Rony Irawanto, \u201cManagement of Botanical Gardens in Indonesian Plant Conservation,\u201d, SEMSINA 2023: 322 \u2013 7 329. DOI: https://doi.org/10.36040/semsina.v4i01.8116. Angus Nurse, Animal Harm - Perspectives on Why People Harm and Kill Animals (New York: Routledge, 2016), 46.8 Ministry of Environment and Forestry, A Snapshot of Illegal Wildlife Trade in Indonesia (Jakarta: Perkumpulan 9 SKALA, 2017), 38.",
          "USD23 billion annually, which rivals narcotics and illegal weapons trade. Indonesia alone 10 loses around 9 trillion rupiah annually from wildlife crime. A total of USD261 million is 11 disbursed annually for wildlife crime eradication in 67 African and Asian countries. 12 Wildlife crime is one of the low-risk, but high-gain crimes. Perpetrators are often sentenced to weak sanction, which has not created a deterrent effect. Indonesia is a producing, transit, and destination country for poaching and illegal wildlife trade. This crime is difficult to detect because is well organized and supported by specialist actors. Each stage has its own methods, routes, and markets. The network is not led by one central leader, but by a range of domestic and international specialist actors, from storage, treatment, transportation, manufacturing, industrial production, marketing and retailing of wildlife products. 13 Illegal wildlife trade is no longer carried out conventionally through face-to-face meetings but has used information technology. Currently, perpetrators are utilizing social media platforms, online marketplaces, digital payments, courier services, and anonymization software to communicate, hide their identities, and obtain money without being detected. 14 It is estimated that in 2017 at least 40 percent of wildlife traders in Indonesia switched to online markets. 15 Government efforts to preserve biodiversity began in the New Order era through Law Number 5 of 1990 on Conservation of Natural Resources and Ecosystems (Law 5/1990). Law 5/1990 regulates comprehensive ecosystem protection, from habitat protection to illegal trade. Law 5/1990 consists of 14 chapters and 45 articles. The Law regulates protection of life support systems, preservation of plant and animal species diversity and ecosystems; Sanctuary Reserve, preservation of plant and animal species, sustainable utilization of natural resources and ecosystems, Nature Conservation Areas, utilization of plant and wild animal species, public participation, handover of affairs and assistance tasks, investigation, and criminal provisions. Law 5/1990 has been the main legal basis in biodiversity management for three decades and is considered necessary to be adjusted to current condition. The urge to review Law United Nations Development Programme, Reducing Maritime Trafficking of Wildlife: Best Practices for Ports (New York: 10 United Nations Development Programme, 2023), 10. Ministry of Environment and Forestry, A Snapshot of Illegal Wildlife Trade in Indonesia, 48.11 M. Montero, et al., Illegal Logging, Fishing, and Wildlife Trade: The Costs and How to Combat it (The World Bank, 2019)12 UNODC, Transnational Organized Crime in Southeast Asia: Evolution, Growth and Impact (UNODC, 2019), 119.13 UNODC, 18.14 D. A. Sinaga, \u201cIllegal wildlife trade flourishes online in Indonesia\u201d, accessed July 12, 2026, https://jakartaglobe.id/news/15 illegal-wildlife-trade-flourishes-online-indonesia/.",
