          "Criminal Law and Philosophy 16 (2) (2022): 395-415, doi:10.1007/s11572-021-09623-3.",
          "Procedural independence is the legal conditions inherent in the status of an investigator, which implies a significant degree of legal freedom in terms of exercising the discretionary powers in a criminal proceeding. This condition is expressed in the ability of the investigator to independently perform any investigative measures, make a variety of procedural decisions, assess the strength of the evidence, and take personal responsibility for their proper execution. 28 The presence of civil servant investigator is crucial to protect the implementation and integrity of a sectoral law. Almost every sectoral law has provisions regarding the mandates for civil servant investigator to conduct investigations. Civil servant investigator has a better Table 4. New Coordination Pattern between Civil Servant Investigator and Police Investigator. New Coordination Pattern Legal Basis In executing pre-investigative duties, civil servant investigator are coordinated, supervised and given instructions by police investigators Article 20 verse (1) Civil servant investigator and specific investigators in carrying out their duties and authorities are under the coordination and supervision of police investigators Article 7 verse (3) Civil servant investigator and specific investigators in carrying out their duties and authorities are required to coordinate with police investigators until the case files are handed over to the prosecutor Article 7 verse (4) The submission of case files carried out by civil servant investigator and specific investigators is carried out through police investigators who then jointly submit the case files to the prosecutor Article 8 verse (3) If civil servant investigator or specific investigators stops the investigation, is obliged to involve police investigator Article 24 verse (3) Civil servant investigator and specific investigators cannot make arrests except on the orders of police investigators Article 93 verse (3) Civil servant investigator and specific investigators cannot make detention except on the orders of police investigators Article 99 verse (3) Source: Criminal Code, Criminal Procedural Code, and Law on Criminal Adjustment S. B. Rossinskiy and D. B. Bazarova, \u201cProcedural Independence of the Investigator: Myth of Reality?\u201d, The 28 American Journal of Political Science Law and Criminology 5(06) (2023): 44\u201356, https://doi.org/10.37547/tajpslc/ Volume05Issue06-09.",
          "understanding of the characteristics of a sectoral criminal act. The effectiv eness of the 29 criminal justice system is largely determined by the alignment between the authority structure and institutional functions. When authority is not placed in the hands of the most substantively competent actors, the system will operate inefficiently and be costly. In this context, the subordination of the civil servant investigator under police investigator negates the initial purpose of its establishment to accelerate sectoral law enforcement. 30 Identification of Strategic Actions to Accelerate Law Enforcement of Wildlife Crimes The Indonesian government in 2015 took a progressive step in eradicating wildlife crimes by forming specialized unit, namely the Directorate General of Law Enforcement on Environmental and Forestry at Ministry of Environment and Forestry. Over the past decade, the Ministry of Environment and Forestry (currently the Ministry of Forestry) has successfully carried out 533 operations to eradicate illegal wildlife trafficking and resolved 480 criminal cases. From the results of these operations, 256.451 animals and 22.857 animal body parts were successfully secured.",
          "Source: statistic data of Ministry of Environment and Forestry 2015 \u2013 2024. Various policy breakthroughs in Law 5/1990 and 32/2024 need to be strengthened with several strategic actions to accelerate law enforcement. The study identified three strategic actions in law enforcement, namely the application of layered articles and multi-regime Graphic 1. Law Enforcement on Wildlife Crime by Ministry of Environment and Forestry 2015 \u2013 2024. 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 Operation Criminal Cases F. A. Ndaru, \u201cShould Civil Servant Investigators Become Functional Positions?\u201d, Jurnal Bina Praja 16(3) (2024): 29 639-661, https://doi.org/10.21787/jbp.16.2024.639-661. T. W . Pranoto, et al., \u201cThe Role of Civil Servant Investigators in the Criminal Justice System Following the 30 Amendment of the Criminal Procedure Code\u201d, JUSTLAW: Journal Science and Theory of Law 2(2) (2025): 1-11, e- ISSN: 3089-2414.",
          "investigations, science and technology support, and collaborative networks at the international level. The first strategic action is application of layered articles and multi-regime investigations. From 29 court decisions related to wildlife crimes from January to August 2020 collected by the WildEye community organization, it was concluded that the sentences for perpetrators of wildlife crimes were still very low. More than half of the cases were only sentenced to less than a year in prison. The data also shows that the fines are relatively minimal. Seven defendants were sentenced to a fine of IDR100 million, five cases with a fine of IDR50 million, while the rest varied between IDR1 million and IDR10 million. 31 One method believed to be effective is the application of layered articles. This method aims to increase deterrent effect using multiple laws. This is done to maximize the application of sanctions for creating deterrent effect and accountability for perpetrators. The advantages of one law can complement the gaps in other laws. Law 5/1990 jo. Law 32/2024 is the core regulation in conservation purview. Wildlife crimes are cross-sectoral, so they not only violate conservation law, but also other rules. In addition to Law 5/1990 jo. Law 32/2024, the study found other regulations that can be utilized to eradicate wildlife crimes, namely Law on Forestry; Law on Environmental Protection and Management; Law on Prevention and Eradication of Forest Destruction; Law on Land and Water Conservation; Law on Customs; Law on Protection of Plant Varieties; Law on Fisheries; Law on Spatial Planning; Law on Mineral and Coal Mining; Law on Horticulture; Law on Management of Coastal Areas and Small Islands; Law on Animal Husbandry and Animal Health; Law on Water Resources; Law on Animal, Fish, and Plant Quarantine; Law on Sustainable Agricultural Cultivation System; Law on Prevention and Eradication of Money Laundering; and Law on the Eradication of Corruption. The application of a legal regime other than conservation law was successfully implemented. Ministry of Agriculture in 2019 used Law Number 21 of 2019 on Animal, Fish and Plant Quarantine to ensnare a wildlife trader named Irawan Shia. In 2020, Pekanbaru District Court sentenced Irawan Shia to four years in prison and a fine of IDR1 billion, which was a higher verdict than the verdict in the case using Law 5/1990. La w 32 enforcement agencies, namely the Corruption Eradication Commission, the Police, and the Prosecutor's Office, also need to investigate alleged corruption, because there are state officials who are involved in wildlife crimes. In addition, the Constitutional Court through R. A. Pratama and W . U. N. Zuhra, \u201cQuarantine Law: New Hope in Eradicating Wildlife Smuggling\u201d, accessed July 12, 31 2026, https://www.ekuatorial.com/2020/10/uu-karantina-harapan-baru-dalam-pemberantasan-penyelundupan-satwa/. Anggi Romadhoni, \u201cJudge sentences African lion cub smuggler to four years in prison\u201d, accessed July 12, 2026, https://32 www.antaranews.com/berita/1615370/hakim-vonis-empat-tahun-penjara-penyelundup-bayi-singa-afrika.",
          "Decision Number 15/PUU-XIX/2021 grant investigators in the forestry sector the authority to investigate money laundering crimes. Money laundering investigations are very important to increase the deterrent effect, dismantle the network of criminal gangs, recover assets, and identify beneficiaries. With the implementation of these various legal regimes, law enforcement that intersects with the issue of biodiversity can be handled by investigators from various agencies, including local governments. And since all civil servant investigators are subordinate of police investigators in Criminal Procedural Code framework, it is also important to establish a strong synergy between civil servant investigator institution and the Police. It is crucial to develop a technical regulation, especially for conducting arrest and detention for civil servant investigator under the order of police investigator. The second strategic action is science and technology support. Law enforcement needs to be supported by science and technology to strengthen the evidence. In uncovering cases related to biodiversity, a science and technology approach is carried out by involving experts, forensic support, and digital transformation. These three things are very important to accelerate the process of making accurate decisions. Experts contribute in translating scientific evidence into legal evidence. Several scientific fields are needed in handling biodiversity cases, including mapping and geospatial, spatial planning, microbiology, molecular biology, forensics, wood administration, wildlife utilization, identification of species, public policy, psychology, sociology, criminology, intelligence, economic valuation, agronomy, and other sciences. Forensic support is the application of scientific knowledge for the purpose of resolving legal problems. The function of forensics is to provide reliable, accurate, and timely information to officers. To uncover wildlife cases, the types of forensics used are biological forensics and digital forensics. Illegal circulation of wildlife can be traced through deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) testing at the wildlife origin and final distribution locations. Through biological forensics, the location of the wildlife habitat can be identified. Digital forensics is also needed because today's crimes use communication and information technology. Digital forensics is the process of acquiring, identifying, evaluating, and presenting electronic evidence (information extracted from computers or digital devices). The science and technology approach can only be realized by involving support from key actors, ranging from academics, practitioners, and experts spread across laboratory institutions, universities, private institutions, or government agencies. Artificial intelligence technology can also be a game changer in eradicating wildlife crimes. Artificial intelligence can help officers identify species, verify the contents and documentation of shipments, detect sales via cyberspace, and identify trade patterns. Artificial intelligence thus has great potential to reduce the need for personnel and time to conduct field inspections.",
          "Since 2017, Ministry of Environment and Forestry has carried out a digital transformation to eradicate wildlife crimes by designing cyber patrol program to monitor wildlife trade via internet, social media, and online marketplace. If an illegal transaction is found online, the Ministry will immediately notify the online platform to immediately close the content and seller account; write and submit a request to Ministry of Communication and Information (currently Ministry of Communication and Digital) to immediately close the content and accounts of the sellers; and/or carry out profiling of cyber accounts involved in illegal transactions and immediately enforce the law. A total of 3.980 contents and 256 social media accounts were caught by the cyber patrol and deleted. 33 The third strategic action is collaborative network at international level. Synergistic partnerships at the global level are urgently required because biodiversity issues are cross- border, have broad and systemic impacts, and involve transnational actors. Understanding and collaboration between countries are unavoidable, both multilaterally and bilaterally, including by using cooperation through mutual legal assistance (MLA) to prosecute transnational criminals. At the global level, there is the International Consortium on Combating Wildlife Crime (ICCWC) which is a coalition of five international organizations, namely the Secretariat of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES), The International Criminal Police Organization (INTERPOL), the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC), the World Bank Group (WBG), and the World Customs Organization (WCO). Indonesia plays an active role in CITES. There are three agencies assigned for CITES, namely: Directorate General of Natural Resources and Ecosystem Conservation as Management Authority; Directorate General of Law Enforcement as Enforcement Authority; and National Research and Innovation Agency as Scientific Authority. Indonesia also plays an active role in regional level instruments such as the ASEAN Wildlife Enforcement Network (ASEAN-WEN) and the ASEAN Senior Officials on Forestry (ASOF). International collaboration is also necessary to obtain funding support through the Global Environment Facility (GEF). GEF is a funding mechanism established in 1991 to foster international cooperation in addressing global environmental threats. GEF is an incremental funding mechanism from the basic financing of recipient countries. GEF prioritizes funding support to ensure the success of strategic steps in building national capacity in supporting environmental protection. Indonesia is among the top five countries that can utilize GEF funding and provide real impacts. The GEF-8 cycle (GEF-8 replenishment) has started from July 1, 2022 to June 30, 2026 with funding sources coming Irsyan Hasyim, \u201cCombating Wildlife Trade on the Internet, KLHK Removes 3,980 Content and 256 Accounts\u201d, accessed July 33 12, 2026, https://www.tempo.co/lingkungan/perangi-perdagangan-satwa-di-internet-klhk-hapus-3-980-konten-dan-256- akun-1175980.",
          "from 39 donor countries. Meanwhile, the Country System for Transparent Allocation of Resources (STAR) was published on July 1, 2022. In the 8th cycle of the GEF (GEF-8), Indonesia received a STAR fund allocation of USD 103,65 million, which is divided into 3 focal areas, namely biodiversity (USD82,15 million), climate change (USD20,05 million), and land degradation (USD1,45 million). Besides GEF mechanism, the government can seek funding from bilateral cooperation. Indonesia and Norway partnership can be an interesting example. The two countries are involved in a results-based contribution scheme for emission reductions. The value that has been paid is USD156 million, which is divided into three stages, namely stage 1 (2016/2017), stage 2 (2017/2018), and stage 3 (2018/2019). The two countries are currently discussing further implementation of stage 4. Numerous wildlife rescue initiatives on a global scale have been carried out by Ministry of Environment and Forestry together with related parties. From 2015 to 2024, there have been four cases of wildlife trade cases across countries. The first case was the use of MLA instrument between Indonesian and Dutch authorities to ensnare Eric Roer, a Dutch citizen living in Bali, Indonesia. Because the case was in two different jurisdictions, the MLA was used to help gather information related to this case. This MLA was the first between Indonesia and Netherlands for environmental case. The parties involved in handling this case were Ministry of Environment and Forestry, Indonesian Police, Ministry of Law and Human Rights, and Dutch Police. Denpasar District Court on November 13, 2019, declared Eric Roer guilty of distributing body parts of protected animals from Indonesia to Netherlands and sentenced him to two years inprisonment and IDR50 million fine. The second case is the facilitation from Ministry of Environment and Forestry to London Metro Police regarding the case involving celebrity Stephanie Scolaro, a British citizen. The perpetrator distributed hats and bags made of rare snakeskin from Indonesia to Germany worth \u00a318,000. To deceive officers, the perpetrator used fake CITES documents. On January 22, 2019, Southwark Crown Court sentenced Stephanie Scolaro to 12 months of community service and 160 hours of unpaid work. London Metro Police expressed their gratitude to the Indonesian Government for the support at the London Conference on the Illegal Wildlife Trade 2018. The third case is the repatriation of 322 rare birds in collaboration with the Vietnamese authorities. On June 17, 2019, the Vietnamese police confiscated 322 parrots without valid documents at Noi Bai Airport. The sender of the birds was a logistics company with an address in North Sumatra Province, Indonesia. The Vietnamese police then asked for assistance from the Indonesian Police to repatriate the birds (312 birds were dead and 10 were still alive). In July 2019, the Indonesian Police asked for support from Ministry of",
          "Environment and Forestry for the repatriation process and joint investigation in uncovering the wildlife crime network. The fourth case is the repatriation of 91 endangered Indonesian animals from Davao, Philippines. These animals were confiscated by the Philippine Authorities since April 8, 2019. On October 14, 2019, the Maty City Court ordered the Philippine Government to repatriate the animals to Indonesia. On July 30, 2019, the repatriation process was held in Bitung City, North Sulawesi Province, Indonesia and was attended by the Mayor of Bitung, the Ministry of Transportation, the Agricultural Quarantine Agency, the Directorate General of Customs and Excise, the Masarang Foundation, and the mass media."
